{"id":"85854511-0f0c-4cdb-86df-44452f95579e","object":"chat.completion","created":1785016512,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_of = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children_of[p].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n\\n    retained = set(id_to_node.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    # Count occurrences of each required symbol in all nodes\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        s = n['symbol']\\n        if s in required_symbols:\\n            symbol_counts[s] = symbol_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n\\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable tie-break)\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any child remains retained\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children_of[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbols after removal\\n        symbol = n['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            if symbol_counts.get(symbol, 0) <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        # Remove node\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1\\n\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained))\\n    removed_node_ids = list(removed_order)  # already in removal order\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_node_ids,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function `solve(case)` that takes the payload (the inner object) and returns an object with `removed_node_ids` (list of strings) and `retained_node_ids` (list of strings). The task is to perform grammar-guided node reduction based on the given description.\n\nThe case has:\n- nodes: list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- required_symbols: list of symbols that must be preserved.\n\nWe need to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes (removable: true) while ensuring:\n- Root (presumably id \"root\") and nodes with required symbols are not removed.\n- A node with retained children cannot be removed (if children are not removed, parent can't be removed? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node that has any child that is retained cannot be removed. That is a condition.)\n- Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. So before removing a node, check that the required symbols still appear somewhere in the tree after removal.\n- Use deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., process nodes in decreasing depth). Among same depth, use node ID as tie break (stable order).\n- The removal order should be the actual order in which we decide to remove nodes.\n- Return sorted retained IDs (presumably alphabetical or stable sort?) and the actual removal order (the order we removed them, not sorted). The output schema says \"removed_node_ids\" array (order? likely removal order) and \"retained_node_ids\" array (presumably sorted). The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained should be sorted (alphabetically or by ID order? Usually stable sort by string). We'll sort retained IDs alphabetically. Removed IDs in order of removal.\n\nWe need to implement algorithm:\n1. Build a tree from nodes. Use parent field. Root's parent is null.\n2. Identify root node.\n3. Build mapping from id to node.\n4. Build children mapping.\n5. Identify required nodes: those whose symbol is in required_symbols. Those cannot be removed.\n6. We will process nodes in order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (stable tie-break). Actually deeper first, then ID order.\n7. For each node in that order (excluding root and required nodes), check if it is removable. If not, skip.\n8. Check if any of its children are retained (i.e., not removed yet). If it has any child that is not removed, then cannot remove because it has retained children.\n9. Check if after removing this node, all required symbols still exist in the remaining tree. This means we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed (including possibly this node itself if its symbol is required? But required nodes are never removed anyway). Since we only remove nodes that are not required, and we only remove a node if all required symbols remain represented. That means we need a way to check: After removal, does the set of symbols in retained nodes still include all required symbols? So we need to maintain a count of required symbols in the current retained set. Initially all nodes are retained. When we consider removing a node, we temporarily consider removing it. We need to check that for each required symbol, there is at least one node (other than this one if it carries that symbol) still retained. But since required nodes themselves are never removed, and they carry required symbols, we only need to worry if this node is the only one representing a required symbol. However, the spec says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we should check that after removal, each required symbol still appears in the set of retained nodes. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol in the current retained set. When we remove a node, if it contributed a required symbol, we decrement that count; if any count becomes zero, we cannot remove.\n10. If all checks pass, we remove the node: mark it as removed, add its id to removal order list.\n11. After processing all nodes, the retained nodes are those not removed. Sort their ids (alphabetical) and return.\n\nImportant: The check \"node with retained children\" means if the node has any child that is still retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the node. That implies we process from bottom up, so deeper nodes are considered first. That ensures children are removed before parents if possible. But we still need to check that condition.\n\nEdge cases: Nodes might have multiple children. Some children may have been removed earlier.\n\nWe also need to consider that \"removable\" field indicates if the node is candidate for removal.\n\nThe root is never removable? It has removable: false. So we don't process it.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Parse input: case = payload dict.\n- nodes = case['nodes']\n- required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n- Build dict: id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n- Build children set: children_of = {id: [] for id in id_to_node} then for each node if parent not null, add child id to parent's list.\n- Initialize retained set: all ids initially.\n- removed_order = []\n- Process nodes sorted by: (-depth, id). Since depth is int, we can sort by (n['depth'], n['id']) but we want deeper first, so sort by (-n['depth'], n['id']).\n- For each node in that order:\n    if node['removable'] == False: continue\n    if node['id'] == root_id: continue # root is not removable anyway but just in case\n    # Check required symbol condition: if node['symbol'] is in required_symbols, we need to ensure there is another node with that symbol. but since required nodes themselves are not removable, this node might be the only one of that symbol if it's not required node? Actually required symbols are the symbols that must be present. Nodes with those symbols might be removable only if there is another node with that symbol still retained. The spec says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"a required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is required? Or the symbol itself? It says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" I interpret \"a required symbol\" as \"a node that has a symbol that is required\"? Or \"a node that is required\"? Actually required_symbols are symbols, not nodes. So a node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed? That would be too strict because the required symbols list might be small, but there could be multiple nodes with same symbol; we might remove one if another remains. The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That is ambiguous. But then later: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies a node with a required symbol can be removed if the symbol is still represented elsewhere. So the \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be misinterpretation. The safe approach is: we do not automatically block removal of nodes with required symbols; we only check that after removal, all required symbols remain. That's consistent with the later condition. So we will not hard-block nodes with required symbols; we'll check the representation condition.\n\n- Check children: if any child of this node is still retained (i.e., child id in retained set), then cannot remove. Because if a child is retained, then this node has retained children. So condition: if any child id in retained_set: skip.\n- Check required symbols: compute current counts of required symbols in retained set (excluding this node). We need to know for each required symbol, how many nodes with that symbol are retained. Initially, we can compute a dict: symbol_counts: for each required symbol, count of nodes with that symbol (all nodes). Then when we remove a node, if its symbol is required, decrement count. So we can maintain that map. Before removal, we check if the node's symbol is required and the count after removal would be <1. If so, skip.\n- If both conditions pass, remove: remove id from retained_set, add to removed_order, and update symbol_counts if needed.\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained_set)). Sort alphabetically (string sorting). removed_node_ids = removed_order (as is).\n\nReturn dict: {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nCheck: The nodes have \"utility\" but we are not using it. The description mentions \"utility\" but the algorithm seems to be about removal order based on depth and tie-break on ID, not utility. So we ignore utility.\n\nWe need to handle cases where removal might cause parent to become removable later? But we process depth order, so it's fine.\n\nPotential issue: If a node is removable, but it has removable children that haven't been processed yet? Since we process deeper first, children will be processed before parents. So when we get to a parent, its children might already be removed, so condition \"has retained children\" would pass if all children removed. That's correct.\n\nEdge case: The root may have removable children? Root is not removable, but its children might be removed if conditions satisfied. That's fine.\n\nNow, we implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate the input case.\n\nNow, produce the JSON response with implementation.\n\nWrite the code as a string. Use proper escaping.\n\nThe output must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source string.\n\nI'll write the code with comments for clarity but not necessary.\n\nMake sure to import json? Not needed; just return a dict.\n\nLet's generate the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":2569,"total_tokens":47